elaine: Heads-up: intermittent parse errors in our node sample app when reconnecting mid-stream. Looks like heartbeat/empty `data:` events end up inside the JSON payload after reassembly. Repro steps below.

elaine: Repro (short): start long stream -> force a short network drop -> client auto-reconnect -> occasionally get JSON.parse failures. Happens on mac + CI linux. Browser SDK hasn't hit it yet.

yusuf: Which node-sdk version? Any sample-app custom code?

elaine: node-sdk v0.10.x, sample-app untouched. I captured a raw stream snip: 

```
data: {"part":"A"}

data:

data: {"part":"B"}

```

elaine: After our naive reassembly the client sometimes sees `{\"part\":\"A\"}\n{\"part\":\"B\"}` as a single `data:` payload and parsing fails.

tomas: That smells like buffer boundary reassembly + our `.split('\n\n')` + `.join('')` logic. If a keepalive is `data:\n` and it gets split, joining erases the separator.

yusuf: Also check CRLF vs LF. Some proxies send `\r\n`. Our parser should normalize `\r` early.

elaine: Current handler in sample-app (simplified):

```js
let buf = '';
res.on('data', c => {
  buf += c.toString();
  const events = buf.split('\n\n');
  buf = events.pop() || '';
  events.forEach(e => {
    const data = e.split('\n').filter(l => l.startsWith('data:')).map(l => l.slice(5)).join('');
    try { handle(JSON.parse(data)); } catch (err) { console.error('parse failed', data); }
  });
});
```

elaine: Quick local patch I tried: normalize CRLF to LF and `join('\n')` instead of `join('')`, and treat whitespace-only assembled data as heartbeat. It cut flakes in half.

`tomas`: Sounds right. We should add a canonical `parseEventStream` util in node-sdk and make sample-app use it; then add unit tests that fuzz chunk boundaries (split inside `data:` token, split between `\r` and `\n`, etc.).

yusuf: For parity, add the same tests for python and browser SDKs so we don't diverge. Also add a small fuzzer in sdk-stream-tests that feeds random chunk splits to the parsers.

